Yes. In load balancing, these are called load-balancing algorithms / request-distribution algorithms. The most common ones are:

| Algorithm                          | How it works                                                                  | Best use case                     |
| ---------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| **Round Robin**                    | Sends requests sequentially: Server 1 → 2 → 3 → 1...                          | Servers have similar capacity     |
| **Weighted Round Robin**           | More powerful servers get more requests                                       | Servers have different capacities |
| **Least Connections**              | Sends request to the server with the fewest active connections                | Long-lived connections            |
| **Weighted Least Connections**     | Least-connections algorithm, but considers server weights/capacity            | Different server capacities       |
| **IP Hash**                        | Hashes client IP and maps it to a server                                      | Need client → same server         |
| **Sticky IP / Source IP Affinity** | Same client IP is consistently routed to the same backend                     | Session persistence               |
| **Least Response Time**            | Routes to server with the lowest response time, often considering connections | Performance-sensitive systems     |
| **Random**                         | Selects a server randomly                                                     | Simple/basic distribution         |
| **Weighted Random**                | Random selection based on server weights                                      | Different server capacities       |
| **Resource-Based**                 | Chooses server based on CPU, memory, load, etc.                               | Dynamic environments              |
| **Consistent Hashing**             | Hash-based routing designed to minimize reassignment when servers change      | Distributed systems/caches        |
| **Power of Two Choices**           | Randomly selects two servers and chooses the less-loaded one                  | Large-scale systems               |
| **Geographic / GeoDNS**            | Routes users to a server/region based on location                             | Global applications               |

1. Round Robin

Suppose you have:

LB
 ├── Server A
 ├── Server B
 └── Server C

Requests:

Request 1 → A
Request 2 → B
Request 3 → C
Request 4 → A
Request 5 → B
Request 6 → C

Very simple and commonly used.

2. Weighted Round Robin

Suppose:

Server A → Weight 5
Server B → Weight 3
Server C → Weight 2

Approximately:

A → A → A → A → A
B → B → B
C → C

So A receives more traffic because it has more capacity.

3. Least Connections

The load balancer checks active connections:

Server A → 20 connections
Server B → 8 connections
Server C → 15 connections

New request:

          ↓
         LB
          ↓
       Server B

Because B currently has the fewest connections.

This is particularly useful when requests don't all take the same amount of time.

4. Sticky Session / Session Affinity

The idea is:

User A → Server 1
User A → Server 1
User A → Server 1

User B → Server 2
User B → Server 2

The load balancer tries to keep a client on the same backend.

This can be implemented using things such as:

Cookie-based affinity
Source IP affinity
Application/session identifiers

Important: Sticky sessions can become a problem if one server receives many users.

5. IP Hash

The load balancer calculates something like:

hash(client_IP) % number_of_servers

For example:

192.168.1.10 → hash → Server A
192.168.1.20 → hash → Server B
192.168.1.30 → hash → Server C

The same IP will generally map to the same server as long as the backend topology doesn't change.

IP Hash ≠ exactly the same thing as sticky sessions, although both can provide session persistence.

6. Least Response Time

Suppose:

Server A → 100 ms
Server B → 40 ms
Server C → 80 ms

The LB can prefer:

Request → Server B

because B is responding fastest.

This is useful when server performance varies dynamically.

7. Resource-Based Load Balancing

The LB can consider:

Server A
CPU: 80%
Memory: 70%

Server B
CPU: 30%
Memory: 40%

Server C
CPU: 50%
Memory: 50%

It will prefer Server B because it has more available resources.

This is more common in sophisticated/cloud/container environments.

8. Consistent Hashing

This is especially important in distributed systems and caching.

Instead of simply:

hash(key) % N

consistent hashing places servers and keys on a hash ring.

The big advantage is that when a server is added/removed, only a relatively small portion of keys need to move.

Commonly relevant to:

Redis clusters
Distributed caches
Distributed databases
CDNs
Large-scale distributed systems
The ones you should know for a Java/Spring Boot interview

I'd prioritize these:

1. Round Robin
2. Weighted Round Robin
3. Least Connections
4. Weighted Least Connections
5. IP Hash
6. Sticky Session / Session Affinity
7. Least Response Time
8. Consistent Hashing
9. Resource-Based

And understand this distinction particularly well:

Round Robin
     ↓
Equal distribution

Weighted Round Robin
     ↓
Distribution based on server capacity

Least Connections
     ↓
Based on current active connections

IP Hash
     ↓
Based on client IP

Sticky Session
     ↓
Keep a client/session on the same backend

Least Response Time
     ↓
Based on server performance

Consistent Hashing
     ↓
Stable key → server mapping even when nodes change

For a Spring Boot + Microservices architecture, these algorithms commonly come into play around an API Gateway / Load Balancer,
with tools such as NGINX, Spring Cloud LoadBalancer, Kubernetes Services, or cloud load balancers.